WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Hello everyone and welcome to the LangFocus channel.
My name is Paul.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.660
This is another quick video about a linguistic concept.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.420
This one is about something called "Diglossia".

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.120
This word "Diglossia" originated from a Greek word
meaning a state of being bilingual.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.140
"Diglossia" is a situation in which a community
uses two different languages

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.060
or two significantly different varieties
of one language for different situations

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.699
The way it typically works is that one dialect or language
is used for a casual every day interaction

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:32.640
and a different standard language is used
for more official, formal purposes.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.680
The dialect or language that is used
for daily communication is typically a "Low Variety",

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.920
meaning that it is not formally learned

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.840
and the speakers don't formally learn
its grammar or how to write in that language.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.940
It's merely learned as a spoken language.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.440
The language used for more formal situations 
is the "High Variety"

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.460
It is the language that is learned at school, formally.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.060
And speakers learn how to write in that language and they 
learn the formal grammar rules of that language.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.460
In many cases, the community considers this "High Variety" 
to be the "pure" or "correct" form of the language

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.400
and they want to keep it the same 
and not let it change.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.719
The Low Variety, on the other hand, is more flexible 
and it's more likely to change over time

00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:12.739
because it is not restricted 
by the rules of the High language.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.200
"Diglossia" is very common around the world.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.840
The most commonly discussed example
is probably the Arabic language.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:27.379
In every Arabic country, there are different dialects
that differ in grammar, vocabulary and pronounciation
from other dialects but also from Standard Arabic.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.480
Standard Arabic is a very codified language, 
which is basically fixed in its rules

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.880
and doesn't really change 
while the dialects are constantly evolving.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.220
In casual speech with other locals, 
Arabs exclusively use their local dialect.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:44.079
But in writing, in formal speeches, 
and the news broadcasts, Standard Arabic is used.

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:49.339
And Standard Arabic is also used as a "bridge language" 
when communicating with speakers from other dialects.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.040
But really only when necessary,
only when there is some problem communicating.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:58.480
Another example is in Italy, where there are numerous 
regional languages that function as the "Low Variety"

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.620
while Standard Italian functions as the "High Variety".

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.460
These regional languages 
are indeed distinct languages.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.700
Even though they are Romance Languages
that are related to Italian, they are different languages.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.120
But they are often refered to as dialects 
because they are not formally learned at school.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.420
In recent times, these regional languages 
have faded to some degree.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.020
But "Diglosia" is still very common in Italy.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.120
There are lots of people who speaks a regional language 
at home  or with their close friends.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:27.759
But with people who are less familiar 
and in formal situations, they speak Standard Italian.

00:02:27.759 --> 00:02:31.959
Another example is the German language,
which has one "High Variety", Standard German.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.940
and many different "Low Varieties".

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.400
Inside Germany, a lot of the "Low Varieties" 
have disappeared or are starting to fade.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.780
But one interesting example is in Switzerland.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.160
In Switzerland, Diglossia is almost entirely a distinction

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.120
between spoken communication on the one hand
and writing on the other.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.260
People in Switzerland don't really accept the idea
of their dialects being a "Low Variety".

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.719
So they tend to use their dialect in all spoken situations,
even informal situations.

00:02:56.719 --> 00:02:59.460
And Standard German is used only 
for reading and writing.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.099
For people who grew up with no real distinction
between a "Low Variety" and a "High Variety",

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:08.499
Diglossia might seem like a bizarre situation,
that's difficult to wrap their head around.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.380
But languages and the way we use them
are fascinating and full of mystery.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.620
The concept of Diglossia is one that will definitely
come up in a lot of my future videos.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:20.360
So I wanted to make a quick little video about it
so that you are familiar with the concept.

